---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getCollection, render } from 'astro:content';

const themes = (await getCollection('research')).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
const renderResults = await Promise.all(themes.map(t => render(t)));

const highlights = (await getCollection('researchHighlights')).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
---
<Base title="Research">
  <section class="section" style="position:relative; overflow:hidden;">
    <div class="decor-shapes"></div>
    <div class="container" style="position:relative; z-index:1;">
      <span class="eyebrow">Research</span>
      <h1>Deciphering the Function of "Active Proteins" in Living Systems in Real Time</h1>
      <div class="research-intro">
        <p>
          Even after two decades of the complete human genome draft, we still do not know the
          function of many proteins. The genomic, transcriptomics and standard proteomics
          technologies have failed to provide information on the function of many proteins. This
          is mainly attributed to the reason that the function of "active protein' is regulated
          at multiple levels such as irreversible post-translational modification, reversible
          interaction of active proteins with endogenous inhibitors, and targeted protein
          degradation through proteasomes. The ABPP-MS method addresses these limitations posed
          by major "omics" technologies and helps to decipher the function of many proteins,
          which are involved in various human diseases. ABPP-MS has succeeded as a robust mature
          technology over the last decade. Because of its unparalleled strength, many academic
          laboratories and pharmaceutical/biotech industries routinely use ABPP-MS for target
          identification, target validation, and inhibitor screening. However, the existing
          ABPP-MS method uses fractionated proteomes from cells/tissues for downstream analysis.
          Because of this shortcoming, cells and tissues are subjected to sample homogenization
          and this leads to a loss of spatial information on protein activity. In addition, the
          current ABPP-MS method cannot be used to detect the function of protein directly from
          living systems in real-time in a longitudinal manner. To address these issues, our
          research group initiated a research program to study the function of "active proteins"
          with exquisite specificity in living systems in real-time with high spatio-temporal
          resolution.
        </p>
        <p>
          This grand challenge can be broadly divided into two major challenges: the first
          challenge is the development of a highly specific imaging method that can be used to
          follow a particular biological process at the system level with very high spatial and
          temporal resolution and the second challenge is the delivery of chemical
          probes/imaging agents to the target organ/tissue, in particular to the right cell type
          and the organelle of interest in living systems. This is an important problem to be
          addressed as the most advanced chemical probes/imaging agents do not work in living
          animals because of sub-optimal pharmacokinetics (PK), biodistribution, and toxicity
          profiles (Figure 1). In essence, this grand challenge consists of two important
          research problems to be addressed i.e. (i) development of advanced non-invasive
          specific imaging methods and (ii) development of next-generation programmable
          sophisticated delivery systems (iii) finally integration of these two methods to yield
          mature sophisticated biomedical technology.
        </p>

        <figure class="research-figure">
          <img src="/images/research/fig1-convergence-technologies.png" alt="Figure 1" />
          <figcaption class="muted">Figure 1. Integration of novel chemical biology technologies for non-invasive monitoring of a particular biological process in living subjects in real time.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>
          A living cell can be considered as the most sophisticated highly crowded micron-sized
          reaction container encased by a semi-permeable lipid membrane. At any given time, there
          are thousands of physical and chemical transformations that occur in a highly
          coordinated manner. These transformations are mostly carried out by the plethora of
          enzymes and therefore exquisite imaging of particular enzymatic activity is of utmost
          importance. However, the challenge is development of a specific imaging method to
          follow one such transformation in live cells in real time. Apart from this, another
          major challenge is the translation of live cell imaging capabilities to live animal
          imaging. Therefore, it is important to develop a specific imaging method for exquisite
          non-invasive imaging of protein function in live cells and live animals in real time.
          The other challenge is the delivery of chemical probes/imaging agents to the right
          organ/organelle of a living animal. Therefore, it is important to develop safe
          next-generation delivery systems. (Figure 2)
        </p>
        <p>
          The development of synthetic methodology for the modular design of smart delivery
          vehicles that can encapsulate a wide range of cargo is highly demanding. Although, a
          plethora of scaffolds are available based on lipids, carbohydrates, nucleic acids,
          peptides, and synthetic polymers. Most of them do not satisfy all the favorable
          attributes such as modular design, easy synthesis and purification, stimuli-responsive
          behavior, biocompatible, biodegradable, and non-immunogenic, tissue targeting ability,
          and optimal biodistribution, pharmacokinetics, and toxicity profiles (Figure 2). In
          contrast, naturally occurring pseudo-life forms such as viruses through evolution have
          satisfied these stringent requirements. Viruses depend on the host system for their
          replication and therefore they are the most evolved "nanomachines" to deliver their
          cargo with remarkable efficiency and specificity. However, one has to keep in mind that
          this system was perfected after a million years of experimentation through natural
          evolution. Viruses are fascinating nanomaterials because they are made through the
          self-assembly of DNA or RNA with viral coat proteins and most of them are highly
          symmetric and synthesized in perfect monodisperse form. Because of these reasons, there
          were lots of efforts to mimic these structures using custom-designed synthetic proteins
          through computational protein design/genetic methods. Although powerful, this method
          requires sophisticated infrastructure for high-level computation and in addition, the
          success rate of this method is less than 10%. Most importantly, the protein
          nanostructures made through this method lack stimuli-responsive behavior, which is an
          important prerequisite for advanced delivery applications. Therefore, it is important
          to develop alternative technologies for the accurate design of protein-based
          nanoparticles through non-genetic methods.
        </p>

        <figure class="research-figure">
          <img src="/images/research/fig2-characteristics.png" alt="Figure 2" />
          <figcaption class="muted">Figure 2. A. Desired characteristics of advanced delivery systems. B. Desired characteristics of advanced imaging method.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>
          The vision of our laboratory is to develop enabling tools and technologies to address
          these challenges. To achieve these goals, we initiated two independent stand-alone
          research programs in the area of (i) Development of specific imaging methods and, (ii)
          Development of next generation protein-based delivery system. In particular, we are
          currently pursuing two major research programs:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Research Program I (RP-I): Development of specific imaging methods for the accurate detection of enzymatic activity in living systems</li>
          <li>Research Program II (RP-II): Development of enabling chemical technologies for the design of stimuli-responsive synthetic protein nanoassemblies for targeted delivery of pharmacological/imaging agents</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section theme-section">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="eyebrow">Research Themes</span>
      <div class="theme-list">
        {themes.map((theme, i) => (
          <button class:list={["theme-card", { "theme-card--reverse": i % 2 === 1 }]} data-theme-index={i} type="button">
            <div class="theme-card__media">
              <img src={theme.data.image} alt={theme.data.title} />
            </div>
            <div class="theme-card__body">
              <span class="theme-card__index">{`0${i + 1}`}</span>
              <h3>{theme.data.title}</h3>
              <span class="theme-card__cta">Explore this theme →</span>
            </div>
          </button>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="pipeline-rule"><span></span></div>
      <span class="eyebrow">Explore Further</span>
      <div class="highlight-links">
        {highlights.map(h => (
          <a href={`/research-highlights/${h.id}`} class="highlight-link">
            <span>{h.data.title}</span>
            <span class="highlight-link__arrow">→</span>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="theme-dialog" id="theme-dialog" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="theme-dialog__backdrop" id="theme-dialog-backdrop"></div>
    <div class="theme-dialog__panel" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="theme-dialog-title">
      <button class="theme-dialog__close" id="theme-dialog-close" aria-label="Close">×</button>
      <div id="theme-dialog-body"></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div hidden id="theme-data-store">
    {themes.map((theme, i) => {
      const { Content } = renderResults[i];
      return (
        <div data-theme-content={i} data-title={theme.data.title} data-image={theme.data.image}>
          <Content />
        </div>
      );
    })}
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .research-intro { max-width: 78ch; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; font-size: 1.05rem; margin-bottom: 36px; }
  .research-intro p { margin-bottom: 1.3em; }
  .research-intro ul { padding-left: 1.3em; text-align: left; }
  .research-intro li { margin-bottom: 10px; }

  .research-figure { margin: 32px auto; max-width: 78ch; text-align: center; }
  .research-figure img {
    width: 100%;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--color-white);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    padding: 16px;
  }
  .research-figure figcaption {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    margin-top: 10px;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .highlight-links {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: 14px;
    max-width: 85ch;
  }
  .highlight-link {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
    background: var(--color-white);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: 20px 24px;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 0.98rem;
    color: var(--color-navy);
    transition: border-color 0.2s ease, background 0.2s ease;
  }
  .highlight-link:hover {
    border-color: var(--color-teal);
    background: var(--color-bg-alt);
    color: var(--color-teal);
  }
  .highlight-link__arrow { color: var(--color-teal); font-weight: 400; }

  .theme-section { position: relative; }

  .theme-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 48px;
  }

  .theme-card {
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    width: 100%;
    min-height: 480px;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    overflow: hidden;
    cursor: pointer;
    padding: 0;
    background: var(--color-white);
    text-align: left;
    transition: border-color 0.25s ease, box-shadow 0.25s ease, transform 0.25s ease;
  }
  .theme-card--reverse { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }
  .theme-card--reverse .theme-card__media { order: 2; }
  .theme-card--reverse .theme-card__body { order: 1; }
  .theme-card:hover {
    border-color: var(--color-teal);
    box-shadow: 0 26px 60px rgba(27, 36, 71, 0.16);
    transform: translateY(-2px);
  }
  .theme-card__media {
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--color-navy);
  }
  .theme-card__media img {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    transition: transform 0.6s ease;
  }
  .theme-card:hover .theme-card__media img { transform: scale(1.08); }

  .theme-card__body {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 22px;
    padding: 64px 72px;
  }
  .theme-card__index {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: 3.2rem;
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--color-bg-alt);
    -webkit-text-stroke: 1.5px var(--color-teal);
  }
  .theme-card__body h3 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 3vw, 2.5rem);
    line-height: 1.2;
  }
  .theme-card__cta {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 0.98rem;
    color: var(--color-navy);
    transition: color 0.25s ease;
  }
  .theme-card:hover .theme-card__cta { color: var(--color-teal); }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .theme-card, .theme-card--reverse { grid-template-columns: 1fr; min-height: 0; }
    .theme-card--reverse .theme-card__media { order: 0; }
    .theme-card--reverse .theme-card__body { order: 0; }
    .theme-card__media { aspect-ratio: 16 / 9; }
    .theme-card__body { padding: 40px 32px; }
  }

  .theme-dialog {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 200;
    display: none;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: 40px;
  }
  .theme-dialog.is-open { display: flex; }
  .theme-dialog__backdrop {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: rgba(10, 18, 28, 0.65);
  }
  .theme-dialog__panel {
    position: relative;
    background: var(--color-white);
    max-width: 1180px;
    width: 100%;
    max-height: 92vh;
    overflow-y: auto;
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 72px 80px;
    box-shadow: 0 30px 80px rgba(10, 18, 28, 0.35);
  }
  .theme-dialog__close {
    position: absolute;
    top: 22px;
    right: 28px;
    background: var(--color-bg-alt);
    border: none;
    width: 40px;
    height: 40px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    font-size: 1.6rem;
    line-height: 1;
    cursor: pointer;
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }
  .theme-dialog__close:hover { color: var(--color-navy); background: var(--color-border); }

  #theme-dialog-body { max-width: 78ch; margin: 0 auto; }
  #theme-dialog-body img {
    width: 100%;
    border-radius: 3px;
    margin: 18px 0 24px;
  }
  #theme-dialog-body figure {
    margin: 30px auto;
    text-align: center;
  }
  #theme-dialog-body figure img {
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    background: var(--color-white);
    padding: 18px;
    margin: 0 0 10px;
  }
  #theme-dialog-body figcaption {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--color-muted);
    text-align: center;
  }
  #theme-dialog-body h2 { font-size: 0.85rem; letter-spacing: 0.04em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-teal); margin-top: 1.6em; }
  #theme-dialog-body h3 { font-size: 1.05rem; color: var(--color-navy); margin-top: 1.4em; }
  #theme-dialog-body ul, #theme-dialog-body ol { padding-left: 1.2em; }
  #theme-dialog-body li { margin-bottom: 8px; }
  #theme-dialog-body p { margin-bottom: 1em; }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .highlight-links { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    .theme-dialog { padding: 16px; }
    .theme-dialog__panel { padding: 40px 28px; }
  }
</style>

<script>
  const dialog = document.getElementById('theme-dialog');
  const body = document.getElementById('theme-dialog-body');
  const cards = document.querySelectorAll('.theme-card');

  function openDialog(index: string) {
    const source = document.querySelector(`[data-theme-content="${index}"]`);
    if (!source || !body || !dialog) return;
    const title = source.getAttribute('data-title') ?? '';
    body.innerHTML = `<h2 id="theme-dialog-title" style="text-transform:none;color:var(--color-navy);letter-spacing:normal;font-size:clamp(1.4rem,3vw,1.9rem);">${title}</h2>` + source.innerHTML;
    dialog.classList.add('is-open');
    dialog.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'false');
    document.body.style.overflow = 'hidden';
  }

  function closeDialog() {
    dialog?.classList.remove('is-open');
    dialog?.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
    document.body.style.overflow = '';
  }

  cards.forEach(card => {
    card.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const idx = card.getAttribute('data-theme-index');
      if (idx !== null) openDialog(idx);
    });
  });

  document.getElementById('theme-dialog-close')?.addEventListener('click', closeDialog);
  document.getElementById('theme-dialog-backdrop')?.addEventListener('click', closeDialog);
  document.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
    if (e.key === 'Escape') closeDialog();
  });
</script>
